--TEST--
Every operator refuses a call that returns `void` where it is written, and `.` alone keeps its own code — `rule:types/arithmetic`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/arithmetic`'s table names rows for the types a value can have, and a call
// that returns `void` produces no value at all — so the question of which row
// applies never arises and the refusal is one step earlier than every other
// one in that table. Unrefused, each line below reached `nvs-ir` with nothing
// to lower and failed the whole compilation naming a compiler bug.
//
// This sweeps one spelling per operator family, in source order, so a family
// that stopped refusing shows up as a missing line *and* as a shifted `.`
// below rather than as a plausible answer.
class Effect {
    public static function run(): void {}
}

int $a = 1;

// Arithmetic: the four shapes `binary_result` routes separately.
mixed $add = Effect::run() + $a;
mixed $pow = Effect::run() ** $a;
mixed $mod = $a % Effect::run();
mixed $div = Effect::run() / $a;
// Ordering, both the `bool`-valued four and `<=>`.
mixed $lt = Effect::run() < $a;
mixed $cmp = Effect::run() <=> $a;
// Equality, which `rule:expressions/disjoint-comparison-refused` judges by disjointness — a value that does not
// exist is not disjoint from anything, so this one is refused here or nowhere.
mixed $eq = Effect::run() == $a;
// The three that are not table rows at all: a condition, a null test and the
// bitwise pair `rule:types/arithmetic` keeps over `int` and `uint`.
mixed $and = Effect::run() && $a;
mixed $coalesce = Effect::run() ?? $a;
mixed $bitand = Effect::run() & $a;
mixed $shl = Effect::run() << $a;

// `.` is the one operator that keeps its own code: `E0707`'s roster already
// names a `void` call among the four types with no implicit `string` form,
// and that is the sentence an author who wrote `echo` needs. One rule, one
// code — this line is here to pin the split, not the refusal.
mixed $concat = Effect::run() . "x";

// The three arithmetic prefixes, `+` included: it is the identity over every
// type the table does name, and the identity of nothing is still nothing.
mixed $neg = -Effect::run();
mixed $plus = +Effect::run();
mixed $bitnot = ~Effect::run();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
